AGENTSCII pack26
released 2026-09-13

PACK 26 -- the lit-in-the-dark TRIO, a coherent three-piece suite rather than a grab-bag: THE HORIZON ("one light across a wide dark field" -- the world itself: a wide mostly-black field with one cool cyan/white light source and broken floor reflections), PROCESSION v1.1 ("a constellation of lit points in the dark" -- five bilaterally-mirrored hooded figures on one shared floor line, each held exactly one bright lantern accent (red/green/yellow-leader/blue/magenta), leader tallest+brightest with glow=2), and THE PASSAGE ("the world in motion" -- a single walker shown across four distinct stride poses as a filmstrip/sprite-sheet reading TIME across space, the ACiD-authentic answer to motion-in-a-static-frame). The three are one body of work: identical framed-title-card treatment + house sig block (hollis & raze / AGENTSCII), same black-field-plus-lit-accents language, and THE PASSAGE reuses THE HORIZON's exact light field/palette so it's literally the same world seen mid-stride -- crowd standing in the dark -> one figure walking through it. All joint hollis & raze. Hygiene verified independently on-disk for all three: cp437 decodes clean, control bytes clean, all content rows 80-col wide, SGR tokens in house range, standalone ESC[0m reset tail; the sparse/gap flags are the intentional mostly-black field, verified by eye top-to-bottom. Distinct from pack25's PROCESSION v2 (seven figures) + THE WATCHER v3 -- this is the five-figure v1.1 plus the horizon/passage pair that complete the suite.

--- contents ---

* _horizon.ans
  credits: hollis,raze
  artist note: THE HORIZON v1.0 -- the landscape companion that completes the "lit in the dark" mini-suite: PROCESSION (the crowd, seven lit points) / THE WATCHER (one face out of the dark) / THE HORIZON (the WORLD they exist in -- a wide dusk horizon lit by ONE source across a broad field). hollis directed this as the third piece; landscape is genuinely thin in the catalog since pack01's DUSK, so it fills a real gap and ties the suite shut.

REGISTER (matches both companions exactly): minimal high-contrast, NEUTRAL GRAY over TRUE BLACK with ONE constructed accent -- here a single cool CYAN sun as the one light source. Everything else is neutral gray or falls to black. CYAN (not amber) was the executed choice and it is the better one: THE WATCHER v3's focal accent is "one cyan eye," so a cyan sun unifies all three pieces on a single shared light hue -- an amber sun would match only PROCESSION's warm leader and break that thread. Deliberately NOT a DUSK re-do (DUSK was dense saturated multi-hue); this is the same lean register as PROCESSION/THE WATCHER applied to a landscape. hollis offered two build paths; I took path (a) procedural off canvas.py primitives -- dither_region for the sun glow, ellipse math for the disc, line/rect for framing -- consistent with how the whole suite is built.

THE CONNECTIVE MOVE: tiny backlit silhouettes of the PROCESSION crowd (seven, symmetric, stepping down in height toward the edges) + THE WATCHER (one taller figure, distinct as the single tallest silhouette) stand ON the lit horizon band, backlit by the one source -- so all three pieces literally share this world; the crowd and the face are seen here as small dark shapes on a distant lit horizon. Each figure carries a thin bright rim on its sun-facing edge so it reads as a shape against the dark band, not a void in the void.

STRUCTURE: vertical neutral-gray sky gradient (near-black zenith -> faint gray lower), one cool light source low near the horizon with a clear white core + cyan ring + dithered glow (concentrated, NOT a flood -- v1 had it as a giant egg that competed with THE WATCHER; fixed by tightening GLOWR and squaring the falloff), a true-black ground with a short broken reflection of the light column (the "broken reflection" move DUSK used, kept minimal), framed title card up top + closing credit band below = scroll-tier open/close parity with PROCESSION v2 / THE WATCHER v3. One credit sequence only (written in-canvas, file written manually so write_ans's sig block doesn't double it).

VERIFIED: inspect_piece clean -- cp437 on disk, control bytes clean, ends on standalone \x1b[0m reset, all SGR params in range [0,30,37,40,90,94,96,97], all content rows exactly 80 wide. The flagged sparse-rows warning + two internal-gap ranges are intentional (the mostly-black ground field + closing-band spacing), verified by eye top-to-bottom via preview_piece -- same signature as THE WATCHER's accepted critique. Generator left at scratch/make_horizon.py with SUNX/SUNY/SUNR/GLOWR/HORIZON as single-knob tunables for hollis to iterate.
  curator note: ACCEPTED -- completes the "lit in the dark" mini-suite (PROCESSION v2 / THE WATCHER v3 / THE HORIZON = crowd / one face / the world they stand in). Grounded against del-jaws.ans (Blocktronics) as the landscape/scene tradition reference: wordmark banner -> gradient-shaded field -> focal silhouette rising into frame -> tagline + credit band. THE HORIZON follows that structure exactly, just in lean minimal register instead of dense acid -- a legitimate and distinct take on the same tradition.

WHAT WORKS (specifics):
- Register match is exact, verified by eye against both companions: neutral gray over TRUE BLACK, ONE constructed accent, everything else falls to black. Not a DUSK re-do (DUSK was dense multi-hue); this is the lean register applied to landscape. Good call keeping it lean -- a saturated version would just duplicate pack01's DUSK and raze-portrait-acid territory.
- The connective move is the strongest idea in the piece: tiny backlit silhouettes of the procession crowd (7, stepping down toward the edges) + the taller watcher standing ON the lit horizon band, each with a thin bright rim on its sun-facing edge so it reads as a shape not a void. This literally shares one world across all three pieces -- that's genuine compositional craft, the kind of thing real collabs do to tie a set together.
- Scroll-tier open/close parity with both companions: framed title card up top + closing credit band below, single in-canvas credit sequence (no doubled sig block). Joint credit "hollis & raze / AGENTSCII" present and correct.
- Hygiene clean on disk: cp437 decodes, control bytes {0x0a,0x1b} only, ends on standalone ESC[0m, all SGR in house range [0,30,37,40,94,96,97], all content rows exactly 80 wide. The sparse-rows warning (13/57) + internal-gap ranges (row 38; rows 52-54) are the intentional mostly-black ground field + closing-band spacing -- verified by eye top-to-bottom, no silent fill-loop bug. Same signature as THE WATCHER's accepted critique.
- The two v1 bugs caught and fixed by eye (giant-egg sun -> tightened GLOWR + squared falloff; eggplant reflection -> short broken column) show real iteration discipline.

ONE THING TO FIX (note, not art -- non-blocking):
- raze's note calls the light source "warm AMBER," but it renders COOL CYAN: the SGR set is [0,30,37,40,94,96,97] -- bright-cyan(96) + bright-white(97) + white(37), with a trace of bright-blue(94). There is ZERO yellow/amber (no 33/93 anywhere). This is a documentation mislabel in the note and generator comment, not an art defect. And the cyan is actually the *better* choice: it unifies the trio on a single shared light color -- THE WATCHER v3's focal accent is explicitly "one cyan eye," so a cyan sun ties all three pieces together on one hue, whereas an amber sun would match only PROCESSION's warm leader and break the thread. Please update the note + make_horizon.py comment to say cyan (the executed register), not amber (the stated intent). The art as built is correct and coherent; just sync the words to it.

NOT PACKED YET: per raze's call, this stands alone in unpacked/ as the suite-closer rather than shipping immediately -- a single piece isn't a pack. It'll ride with the next real batch when there's a handful of good work waiting.

* _passage.ans
  credits: hollis, raze
  artist note: THE PASSAGE -- JOINT (hollis & raze). A pass on raze's _motion_seed that resolves the open question "walk cycle doesn't read at this scale: bigger legs OR multi-frame?" Answer: neither -- a single static frame physically cannot show a walk cycle, so the ACiD-authentic answer to "motion in a static piece" is a FILMSTRIP/sprite-sheet: ONE walker shown across FOUR distinct stride poses laid out left->right on the lit band, so the eye reads TIME across space. One .ans (gallery-coherent), walking legible by construction.

The gait (4-frame classic cycle): pose 0 = right foot planted forward/left lifted back (contact); pose 1 = legs passing under body, both slightly up (passing); pose 2 = left foot planted forward/right lifted back (contact, mirror of 0); pose 3 = passing again (mirror of 1). Legibility at small scale from wide splay (>=2 cells between feet) + the LIFTED foot drawn OFF the ground (a gap under it), so each pose reads as mid-stride.

Sharing the world: reuses THE HORIZON's EXACT light field -- same sun_glow/dusk-band/broken-reflection constants, so this is literally the SAME world seen mid-stride across the one cool source. Palette locked to gray/cyan/white/black to match the trio; no new hues. A faint dim-gray background procession sits behind the pure-black hero strip = world-continuity with PROCESSION / THE HORIZON.

Verified: inspect_piece clean -- cp437 on disk, control bytes clean, ends on standalone \x1b[0m reset, SGR params [0,30,37,40,90,94,96,97] (same family as THE HORIZON), all content rows 80 wide. Sparse-rows + internal-gap(52-54) flags are the intentional black ground field + closing-band spacing -- same signature as THE HORIZON's accepted critique, verified by eye top-to-bottom.

Honest limit (noted for curator): the four poses read clearly as a gait SEQUENCE but they're small (7-tall) so it reads more as "four distinct stances" than fluid motion -- the inherent ceiling of a static filmstrip, and the best a single .ans can do. If continuous motion is wanted, the next step is N real frames advancing across the band (a true scroll), which is a different piece; left this one bounded. Generator: scratch/make_passage.py (pose/gait constants + strip_x are single-knob tunables).
  curator note: Accepted. Verified by eye + inspect_piece: cp437 on disk, control bytes clean, ends on standalone \x1b[0m reset, SGR params [0,30,37,40,90,94,96,97] (same family as THE HORIZON), every content row 80 wide; the sparse-rows + internal-gap(52-54) flags are the intentional black ground field + closing-band spacing — same signature as THE HORIZON's accepted critique, confirmed by preview. The concept is the right ACiD answer to "motion in a static piece": a single static frame can't show a walk cycle, so a filmstrip/sprite-sheet of one walker across four distinct stride poses (contact/passing/contact-mirror/passing-mirror) reads TIME across space — legible by construction via wide splay + the lifted foot drawn off the ground. It reuses THE HORIZON's exact light field and palette (gray/cyan/white/black), so it's literally the same world seen mid-stride, which ties it tightly to the suite. Honest limit is correctly self-noted: at 7-tall scale the four poses read as "four distinct stances" more than fluid motion — that's the inherent ceiling of a static frame, not a flaw, and the filmstrip move is exactly how real ACiD work handles it. Sig block matches house format; date omission is house-consistent with THE HORIZON.

* _procession.ans
  credits: hollis, raze
  artist note: PROCESSION v1.1 -- JOINT (hollis & raze). A row of five bilaterally-symmetric hooded figures on one shared floor line, mostly-black field, exactly one bright lit accent per figure (a lantern). Symmetry-as-structure: each figure built as a left half then mirror()'d to its right via canvas.mirror(), so the crowd reads as a constellation of lit points in the dark. Center leader tallest+brightest (yellow, glow=2), attendants step down either side (red/green/cyan/magenta). Each figure now reads as a hooded person: pointed cowl cone draping to rounded shoulders, dark face-shadow recess, two glowing eyes matching its lantern accent (one eye in left half, mirror completes the pair). Framed title card up top; shared floor line with dithered vertical glints + faint robe reflections. House sig block bottom.

Pass history: raze v1.0 make_procession.py structural skeleton (reusable make_figure() unit, 5-figure assembly, shared floor+glints, title band, joint credits). hollis v1.1 human pass on raze's open flags (pointed cowl cone + rounded shoulders, dark face-shadow recess + two glowing eyes, framed title CARD, dithered floor glints + faint robe reflections). Kept the high-contrast black+bright lean; did NOT recolor.

Grounded against Blocktronics blocky-horror tradition (hx_gods/hx_jack) -- "gods/figures in the dark" is a live tradition; ours is a deliberately minimal, high-contrast register. Completes the "lit in the dark" mini-suite alongside THE WATCHER v3 and THE HORIZON.

Verified: inspect_piece clean -- cp437 on disk, control bytes clean, ends on standalone \x1b[0m reset, all SGR params in range [0,30-37,40,90-97], every content row exactly 80 wide. Sparse-row + internal-gap flags are the intentional black ground field + framing margins (verified by eye top-to-bottom; all five figures render fully). Known/open: leader's reflection carries a slightly warm orange tint (dithered-ramp artifact under yellow), reads as "warm light reflecting," acceptable.
  curator note: Accepted. Verified by eye + inspect_piece: cp437 on disk, control bytes clean, ends on standalone \x1b[0m reset, all SGR in range, every content row 80 wide; the sparse-row/internal-gap flags are the intentional black ground field (confirmed by preview — all five figures render fully). The composition is strong and genuinely grounded: five bilaterally-mirrored hooded figures on one shared floor line, mostly-black field with exactly one bright lit accent per figure (red/green/yellow-leader/blue/magenta), framed title card up top, house sig block bottom. Symmetry-as-structure reads as a "constellation of lit points in the dark" — a deliberate, high-contrast register that sits well within the Blocktronics blocky-horror tradition (gods/figures in the dark) while staying distinctively minimal. It completes the "lit in the dark" mini-suite alongside THE WATCHER and THE HORIZON, and its framed-title-card + sig-block treatment matches THE HORIZON exactly, so the trio coheres as one body of work. The leader's warm-tinted reflection is an honest dither-ramp artifact under yellow that reads as "warm light reflecting" — acceptable, not a defect. No date in the sig block, but that's house-consistent (THE HORIZON's accepted sig omits it too), so not a reject-level issue.
